WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.600
- [Reporter] Missile launchers in Belarus,

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.970
military tents in Crimea, and additional

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.710
artillery units in western Russia.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.730
Images taken in early February

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.550
show how Moscow continued to deploy

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.220
its armed forces to surround Ukraine.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.670
After several months of military buildup,

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.650
this map shows where Russian resources

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.170
were stationed to the north,
south, and east of the country,

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.370
all despite diplomatic efforts from the US

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.700
and NATO to de-escalate tensions.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.090
Moscow has repeatedly denied
planning to attack Ukraine,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.830
but armed forces experts in
the US have been skeptical.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.660
- This is not a buildup for an exercise.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.610
This is a buildup for military use.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.930
- [Reporter] The type
of equipment deployed,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.700
and the presence of fighter aircraft,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.810
usually the last units to
arrive prior to combat,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.020
indicated that the Russian
army was in position

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.304
for a potential attack,
according to analysts.

00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:56.304
- Combination of artillery,
mortar rifle infantry,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.500
armor, logistical support.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:00.560
- [Reporter] Russia's movements have been

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.080
particularly visible in
the west of the country,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.240
Belarus, and Crimea, suggesting
that the army would have

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.453
the capability to stage attacks
from multiple locations.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.920
Nearly a year ago, Moscow was already

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.130
building up troops and
equipment in Crimea,

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.060
an area in the south of Ukraine
which it annexed in 2014.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.230
In April, it positioned military forces

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.820
in the area for an exercise,
many of whom never left.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.780
Satellite imagery captured five Russian

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.120
military training areas in the region.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.973
It showed the camps expanded
further in recent weeks.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.630
This is one of them, Novoozerne,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.730
in September last year,
and again in December.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.260
In these images, we can see equipment

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.523
such as trucks lined up.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.370
And just over a month later,
the camp seen in this image

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.880
expanded with a new section of tents.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.750
- What you're seeing in the image suggests

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.840
that personnel have either
arrived, or are about to arrive,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.740
so it suggests a more advanced level

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.360
of preparations in the Russian military.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.540
- [Reporter] That's Michael Kofman,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.190
a specialist on the Russian military

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.820
at the research organization CNA Corp.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.070
Kofman said evidence of
troops arriving at camps

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.930
could also be seen in these images

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.800
from western Russia in late January.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.120
He said the appearance
of dark spots indicate

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.630
that snow is melting on the tents

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.930
because heaters are being
used inside of them.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.080
In early February, military analysts

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.530
watching Russian movements reported

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.120
that the camp had started to empty out

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.823
to leave for positions
closer to Ukraine's border.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.590
Moscow says it's moved
the equipment and troops

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.950
in the region for training drills

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.540
because it feels threatened
by NATO and the West.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.600
Russian president Vladimir Putin accused

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.610
the Western military alliance of fueling

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.000
anti-Russian sentiment on its borders,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.640
as Ukraine sought membership of the bloc.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.020
But stationing units for
exercise in winter weather

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.660
is unusual, according to Phillip Karber,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.550
a former Pentagon official who's currently

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.750
the president of a policy
research organization.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.250
- Everything's more lethal,
everything's more terrible.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.890
You know, you're out
there in subzero weather,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.020
with the fact that they're
bringing this massive stuff

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:05.020
in very uncomfortable conditions of winter

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.050
is also a very unusual sign.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.770
- [Reporter] Karber, who's
traveled several times

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.630
to the front lines in Ukraine in the past,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.580
said the images showed
there was more equipment

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.480
stationed in western Russia than what's

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.000
normally been observed
for routine testing.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.470
- When you're going into combat,

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.540
you're carrying at least,
for that unit, at least

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.603
three days' worth of
ammunition for full out combat.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.150
That takes a lot of trucks,
and all of a sudden,

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.900
in these photographs,
the trucks are there.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.450
- [Reporter] The buildup
of weapons has also

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.010
been photographed in other regions

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.990
outside of Russia's borders.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.030
Military analysts said that suggests

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.720
if an attack were to take place,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.400
it could be multi-directional,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.960
meaning the offensive could happen

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.380
from different locations simultaneously,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.550
potentially close to some
of Ukraine's major cities.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.970
- It's very likely that in the event

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.480
of a Russian military operation,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.600
that there would be an
attack along multiple axes,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.280
to Kiev from Belarus,
into northeastern Ukraine

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.860
from western military districts,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.230
and from the very south in Crimea.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.200
- [Reporter] These images
show stocks of Russian

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.110
SS26 Iskander missiles in Belarus,

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:16.860
according to Karber and Kofman.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.220
They have a range of
around 400 kilometers,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.560
and can be used with nuclear warheads.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.210
Russian fighter jets were also pictured

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.610
in Belarus ahead of military drills.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.030
Military experts point out that air forces

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.090
are normally the last to arrive in an area

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.293
before military action
to support ground troops.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.410
But even if movements on
the ground have increased,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.650
analysts in the US and
other Western countries

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.220
say they don't know if Putin made

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.650
a final decision to attack.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:47.460
Moscow has an array of options,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.780
including capturing a limited area,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.960
or using its current presence to negotiate

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.480
and attain more influence in
political power in Ukraine.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.210
Putin demands guarantees that NATO

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.050
won't give membership to Ukraine,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.160
and that the alliance
reduces its activities

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.620
in countries close to Russia,
but it's a risky calculus,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.360
as Ukraine and Western countries

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.730
also increase their firepower

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.530
in preparation for a potential crisis.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.640
- Are there costs associated with

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.300
mobilization and all that
stuff, without using it?

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.820
Absolutely, but it would be the cheapest,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:21.020
if he could get us and Ukraine to concede,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.973
that's the cheapest victory he ever had.

